data language_target_registry_totality_law: String = "NO INVISIBLE REMAINDER (operator requirement C, 2026-08-04). This registry may not read as complete because two specimen rows exist. Every target-bearing surface this repository currently carries is EITHER joined to a registered subject OR appears here as an exact row with no identity, which the join kernel then reports as SurfaceMissingTargetIdentity. A surface that is simply absent from these lists is the one state the wall cannot see, which is why the population definition below is declared rather than implied, and why the TargetVocabulary rows carry an explicit scaffold note rather than a silent transcription. The registry today registers exactly ONE subject — Bash — so nearly every row below is deliberately unidentified. That is the honest starting state, not a defect in the wall."

data language_target_registry_vocabulary_scaffold_note: String = "DECLARED SCAFFOLD, with its dissolution trigger — the TargetVocabulary rows below are HAND-DECLARED, and they were meant to be derived. The intent was to read v2.lens.realization_vocabulary_containment's target_ast_vocab_modules and target_ast_vocab_module_prefixes directly, so a module added to the lens would appear here automatically as an unidentified surface and the second-hardcoded-roster fork would close by construction. THAT IMPORT DOES NOT COMPILE TODAY, and the blocker is not in this module: the lens fails to resolve fold_list and list_snoc_item from v2.std.algebra even when compiled from its OWN source root as the entry — measured 2026-08-04. That is the Class B pool-membership defect DESIGN's import-strip open thread describes: a module's bare cross-module references resolve only when some unrelated import elsewhere in the assembled closure has already dragged the target into the pool, so the lens resolves inside the whole-corpus compile and not in a narrow closure. Transcribing the two lists here would be the workaround DESIGN section 5 forbids — an unmarked second representation of the very roster this registry exists to unfork — so they are declared as a counted scaffold instead. DISSOLVE-ON: the lens resolves under a narrow closure (Class B fix), at which point these rows become the fold over its lists; and ultimately the reverse direction, the lens deriving its confined set FROM this registry, which is the obl-lang-support-registry to obl-lang-no-new-debt-gate prerequisite edge and is not done here."

data language_target_registry_population_definition: String = "Target-bearing surfaces enumerated from six declared authorities: (1) StdLanguageCatalogue — std.languages Language rows; (2) TargetModelDeclaration — committed TargetModel-bearing language modules; (3) EmitHostExecution — tools.emit_host_transport per-language smoke fns; (4) TargetVocabulary — the confined module list and prefix list v2.lens.realization_vocabulary_containment enforces; (5) RuntimeChecker — external toolchains a support claim would execute against; (6) LegacyFrontier — gunbc.plans.language_target_self_host_frontier's prose rows. ALL SIX authorities are HAND-DECLARED at this revision — authority 4 was intended to be derived and is a counted scaffold with a stated trigger (language_target_registry_vocabulary_scaffold_note); nothing in this module is derived from the live tree."

data language_target_registry_maximum_conclusion: String = "MAXIMUM CONCLUSION — what a green totality check here does and does not establish. IT ESTABLISHES: every surface in the declared population above is accounted for, each either joined to a registered subject or reported as an exact unidentified row; IT DOES NOT ESTABLISH that the declared population is COMPLETE with respect to the repository. All six authorities are hand-declared, so a target-bearing surface nobody wrote a row for is invisible to every assertion over this module — the failure mode loyal-ant-382 measured at 43 percent under-selection on a text-keyed census and this session reproduced at 100 percent on the ShellCommand sink. Closing that needs a Node-tree observer deriving the population from the real module tree, which is the residue-census obligation and is NOT built here. No count in this module may be quoted as a corpus claim."

data registry_revision_note: String = "The registry's rows are facts about a tree state, so the observation is anchored: subject_revision names the commit these rows were enumerated against. A row set carried without its revision is a measurement pretending to be a standing fact (DESIGN section 5's oracle rule), and re-reading this module against a later tree is exactly how the vocabulary derivation stays honest while the hand-declared authorities go stale."

data language_target_registry_subject_revision: String = "d8bb8d2e036"

data bash_subject_ref: ExternalSubjectRef = ExternalSubjectRef {
  declaration: DeclarationRef {
    module_path: "extdeps.languages.bash.subject",
    decl_name: "gnu_bash",
    field: WholeDeclaration
  }
}

data d0_population_blocker_note: String = "D0 — THE COMPLETE TARGET UNIVERSE — IS NOT REGISTERED HERE, AND THE REASON IS THE CONSTRUCTION WALL ITSELF. A registration derives its subject from a REQUIRED TargetGrounding, so an ungrounded target is unwritable (target_grounding_law). External grounding is an ExternalModelScope, and a scope carries at least one real upstream citation by construction. Therefore registering TypeScript, C, C++, Verilog, SPICE, Python, Go, Java, Kotlin, Swift, LLVM IR, WebAssembly, PTX, machine code, Lean, SQL, HTML, CSS, ECMAScript, JSON, YAML and Markdown requires FIRST authoring one cited subject declaration each — the same file this branch created for Bash — because NONE of them has one today. That is the measured state, not an obstacle invented here: Bash was uncited until this branch, and the same sweep that found it found no subject declaration for any other target. Registering them without grounding would mean either fabricating citations or reopening the wall, and both are refused. The .dag language and controlled English take ProjectGrounding instead and need no external citation, so they are the cheapest rows to add next. SIZING, so the remaining work is countable rather than vague: roughly twenty external subject declarations, each a new dag/extdeps scope carrier requiring its own roster enrollment, each needing a citation verified against the real upstream rather than recalled. DISSOLVE-ON: those subjects land and this note deletes with the rows it explains."

data project_grounded_targets_note: String = "The two PROJECT-grounded targets, and they are here for a reason beyond being cheap: ProjectGrounding had zero inhabitants, so an entire arm of the grounding coproduct was unexercised. These are also the two rows that could not have existed under a universal ExternalSubjectRef — the .dag language and the controlled-English medium are this project's OWN targets, with no independently governed upstream to cite, and forcing them through an external scope would have made them claim a governance they do not have. Their authority declarations are real and symbolic: dag_item_forms is the .dag surface-form authority, FormalSentence the controlled-English one. Neither carries an extdeps_model_scope and neither should."

data registered_language_targets: List<LanguageTargetRegistration> = [
  LanguageTargetRegistration {
    grounding: ExternalGrounding { scope: extdeps_model_scope },
    disposition: TargetActive
  },
  LanguageTargetRegistration {
    grounding: ProjectGrounding {
      authority: DeclarationRef {
        module_path: "extdeps.languages.dag.syntax",
        decl_name: "dag_item_forms",
        field: WholeDeclaration
      }
    },
    disposition: TargetActive
  },
  LanguageTargetRegistration {
    grounding: ProjectGrounding {
      authority: DeclarationRef {
        module_path: "v2.extdeps.languages.english",
        decl_name: "FormalSentence",
        field: WholeDeclaration
      }
    },
    disposition: TargetParked {
      reason: "controlled-English medium: bounded formal-subset profile, P3 priority; registered so the strategy row stays visible while implementation has not begun"
    }
  }
]

data dag_subject_ref: ExternalSubjectRef = ExternalSubjectRef {
  declaration: DeclarationRef {
    module_path: "extdeps.languages.dag.syntax",
    decl_name: "dag_item_forms",
    field: WholeDeclaration
  }
}

fn registered_target_count() -> Int {
  fold(registered_language_targets, init: 0, f: fn(a, _r) { a + 1 })
}

fn unidentified_decl_row(
  authority: LanguageTargetSurfaceAuthority,
  module_path: String,
  decl_name: String
) -> LanguageTargetSurfaceRow {
  LanguageTargetSurfaceRow {
    authority: authority,
    surface: SurfaceDeclaration {
      ref: DeclarationRef {
        module_path: module_path,
        decl_name: decl_name,
        field: WholeDeclaration
      }
    },
    target: none
  }
}

fn unidentified_module_row(
  authority: LanguageTargetSurfaceAuthority,
  module_path: String
) -> LanguageTargetSurfaceRow {
  LanguageTargetSurfaceRow {
    authority: authority,
    surface: SurfaceModule { module_path: module_path },
    target: none
  }
}

